    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act (ACA) extended health insurance coverage to previously uninsured individuals, thereby altering reimbursement patterns at the hospital level. Given the historical financial difficulties of rural hospitals, this paper focuses on the effect of this insurance expansion on hospital inpatient payer mix and financial outcomes in rural settings. Using hospital-level data from 2006-2017, this paper utilizes a fixed effects regression and difference-in-differences study design to determine the effect of the ACA on rural hospital financial performance in California. I find a statistically significant decline in Medicaid payer mix and increase in private payer mix for rural inpatient services after the ACA. I also find significant reductions in Medicaid income per bed and uncompensated care cost, particularly in uninsured rural areas. However, overall profitability improved in rural hospitals after the ACA, suggesting the financial importance of private shares of payer mix to cover losses incurred by increased expenses of Medicaid patients.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Serrato, Paul Irvin (2019). Health Insurance Expansion and Rural Hospital Finances: Evaluating the Impact of the Affordable Care Act in California. Stanford Digital Repository. Available at: https://purl.stanford.edu/pt830xd1767",
